WEBVTT

00:01:59.781 --> 00:02:03.185
Maybe it's because my dad
was a civil rights attorney

00:02:03.209 --> 00:02:05.464
and my parents were socially conscious

00:02:05.488 --> 00:02:08.290
and demanded that we stand up
for what we believe in.

00:02:08.314 --> 00:02:10.792
Or the fact that my parents
were born in Jamaica,

00:02:10.816 --> 00:02:12.223
came to the United States

00:02:12.247 --> 00:02:15.233
and were able to realize
the American Dream.

00:02:15.257 --> 00:02:17.407
Or maybe it was my residency training,

00:02:17.431 --> 00:02:19.624
where I saw firsthand

00:02:19.648 --> 00:02:22.832
how poorly so many low-income
women of color were treated

00:02:22.856 --> 00:02:24.593
by our healthcare system.

00:02:24.617 --> 00:02:28.377
For whatever the reason,
I felt a responsibility to stand up,

00:02:28.401 --> 00:02:29.855
not just for myself,

00:02:29.879 --> 00:02:31.153
but for all women,